    "turn_idx": 38,
    "turn_text": "More couple than one, I think. Whether it's Richmond, whether it's Charlottesville, whether it's towns across Virginia, and we probably have more memorials and monuments than any state. I think there's going to have to be a local process in every community. And we're going to have to have tough and many ways long overdue conversations about race. And I think Yes, context and questions will come up. I think you'll find as the history has laid out that many of these statues were put up, perhaps not as memorials, but were put up by arch-segregationists in a way to intimidate our fellow Virginians who were of African-American descent. And I think as those stories come out, you're going to end up probably seeing more and more communities take the actions that Charlottesville was in the process of taking. I tell you what I did when I know this touched me in a different way when I was governor. My daughter who was in second grade, we'd walk around Capitol Square and lots of Confederate, former Confederate statues there. And my daughter asked, You know, where's the statue of Rosa Parks? And so my wife gave her credit, quickly said, well, Rosa Parks wasn't from Virginia. So, but we did realize it wasn't a statue at all of any one of color of anything about the part of our history of civil rights. So we started not with taxpayer money, we raised private money and we built the Civil Rights Memorial on Capitol Square, celebrating Barbara Johns who led the walkout at Moten High School in Farmville that led to Brown versus Board of Education. So we try to do our part to tell the whole story of Virginia's history, which is good, bad and the ugly.",
