    "turn_idx": 59,
    "turn_text": "not. We are grinding through interviews, reviewing documents. There's really two things on this, Jimmy. One, the first part. Pretty much everybody agrees with maybe the exception of the president. All the defense department, all the intelligence community, virtually every senator, Democrat, and Republican agrees. You know, the Russians massively intervened in our elections. They broke into both parties, released emails on one candidate. They attacked 21 states, electoral systems. They took Russian agents who created fake accounts and put out news that overwhelmed the search engines on Facebook and Twitter, which then ended up having people read news that was not necessarily real news in certain states. And I'm not looking to relitigate 2016, Trump won. I am looking to make sure that we don't have this kind of Russian intervention happening again in Virginia in 2017 or 2018 across the country. And the fact that the president hasn't acknowledged this means we don't have a national strategy, for example, on how we're going to prevent Russians or some other nation state from interfering in our electoral process going forward. And that is a big issue, a significant issue, and our job on the intel committee is to at least lay out some of the solution set. The second half, which is the more controversial piece, Was there a level of cooperation or collusion between folks affiliated with the Trump campaign and the Russians? I don't know the answer to that yet. There's a lot of questions to be asked. If there's nothing, I'll be the first to say. If there's something the American public deserves to know.",
